        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES: <br>
The response of the cardiovascular system to a quantified workload is a frequently used
index of the general physical condition of an individual. The objective of the Inflight
Exerciser and Work Tolerance (M003) experiment was to evaluate the day-to-day general
physical condition of the Gemini 4 , Gemini 5, and Gemini 7 astronauts during space flight
by evaluating the cardiovascular system. In this examination, variation in heart rate,
blood pressure, and respiration rate to a calibrated workload was studied preflight and
inflight, as the basis for measuring the response of the cardiovascular system. </p>

APPROACH: <br>
The exercise device used for the experiment consisted of a pair of rubber cords. The
cords were attached to a nylon handle at one end and to a nylon foot strap at the other. A
stainless steel cable limited the stretch length of the rubber bungee cords and limited
the isotonic workload of each pull. The device exercised the arms by holding the feet
stationary while the astronaut pulled the handle. In flight, a bioinstrumentation system
was used to collect the cardiovascular data. These data were simultaneously recorded by
the onboard biomedical magnetic tape recorder (for real-time evaluation) and telemetered
to the ground-based monitoring stations. </p>

<p>Exercise periods lasted for 30 seconds, during which time the astronaut stretched the
bungee cords through a full extension one time per second. Ten exercise periods were
performed by subject A of the Gemini 4 mission, while seven exercise periods were
performed by subject B. Exercise periods were performed three times daily by the Gemini 5
astronauts. During the Gemini 7 mission, the exercise was scheduled twice daily.
Additional isometric and isotonic exercises were performed by the Gemini 7 astronauts
approximately three times daily. </p>

<p>Heart rate was determined by counting beats during 15-second intervals for 2 minutes
before and after exercise, as well as during the first and last 15-second intervals during
each exercise. Blood pressure measurements were obtained before and after each exercise
period. </p>

RESULTS: <br>
Comparison of data from preflight exercise periods with data obtained during flight
revealed little difference in heart rate response for all astronauts. In addition,
comparison of data collected from the inflight exercise periods from the first to the last
day of Gemini 5 revealed little difference in heart rate response. Gemini 4 astronauts
exhibited a moderate rise in heart rate during exercise, with a rapid return to near
pre-exercise levels within 1 minute following exercise. The astronauts' heart rates
returned more rapidly to pre-exercise values than did the Mercury 9 astronaut tested (a
34-hour mission). The heart rate of the Gemini 4 astronauts returned to pre-exercise
values 45 to 60 seconds after cessation of exercise. The one astronaut of the Mercury 9
mission had a mean post-exercise heart rate during flight that was slightly increased for
approximately 2 minutes after exercising. However, the environmental control system of the
Gemini spacecraft was superior to that of the Mercury spacecraft; therefore, the data were
not strictly comparable. Evaluation of the heart rate data revealed no significant
difference between the heart rate responses (of both Gemini 4 astronauts) to exercise
during the mission and the responses obtained before the mission. Thus, using the rate of
return of the heart rate to pre-exercise values as an index of physical condition, there
was no evidence of cardiovascular deconditioning at any time during the Gemini 4 mission.
Results were similar for Gemini 5 and 7 astronauts. </p>

<p>During Gemini 4, blood pressure changes were minimal. Generally, blood pressure
measurements, before and after exercise periods on Gemini 5 were not remarkable.
Post-exercise systolic pressures of both astronauts tended to be higher than pre-exercise
values. Generally, post-exercise diastolic pressures were slightly higher than, or were
identical to, pre-exercise values. After the fourth day of flight, both astronauts used
the exerciser frequently between scheduled medical data passes. Both Gemini 5 astronauts
remarked that exercise was essential and beneficial on long-duration space flights. The
results were similar for Gemini 7 astronauts. </p>

<p>On the basis of the data obtained during the missions, investigators concluded the
following: <br>
1) The response of the cardiovascular system to a calibrated workload was relatively
constant for a given individual during space flights lasting up to 14 days. </p>

<p>2) Crewmembers were able to perform mild-to-moderate amounts of work under the
conditions of space flight and within the confines of the Gemini spacecraft. This ability
continued essentially unchanged for up to 14 days in flight. </p>

<p>3) Using a variant of the Harvard Step Test as a cardiovascular deconditioning index,
no decrement in the physical condition of the crewmembers was apparent during space
flights of up to 14 days. No decrement was observed under the stress of the relatively
mild workloads imposed in this experiment. <END> </p>
